  "mechanismSummary": "Corey's aldehyde, the cis-fused lactone carrying an acetate and a CHO, reacts with the sodium salt of dimethyl (2-oxoheptyl)phosphonate. Sodium hydride removes the acidic proton between the P=O and the ketone; the stabilised anion adds to the aldehyde; the alkoxide closes a four-membered oxaphosphetane onto phosphorus; and the ring falls apart to a C=C and sodium dimethyl phosphate. The product is the (E)-enone: the stabilised anion adds reversibly, so the reaction passes through the less crowded intermediate with the two large groups trans (explained, not yet checked by the app). The CHO next to the ring could epimerise in base; the mild, stabilised anion avoids this. Next, Corey reduced the enone ketone to the allylic alcohol with zinc borohydride, removed the acetate and protected both OH groups as THP ethers (not drawn).",
